"""Exercise 3.3, printed pages 81–82, manually checked against JPG and full PDF 45."""
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page
QUESTIONS=[
 r'Find the derivative of the following functions w.r.t. involved independent variable: (a) \(w=4(x^3-4x+2)^5\); (b) \(y=\frac{(4x-x^3)^{11}}5\); (c) \(u=\text{∛}(1-3t^2)\); (d) \(s=\frac1{(3t+1)^7}\).',
 r'Determine the derivative f′(x) in each case: (a) \(f(x)=(2x-5)^3(5x-7)\); (b) \(f(x)=\frac{(x+2)^2}{x-1}\); (c) \(f(x)=\left(\frac{2x-5}{x-4}\right)^4\); (d) \(f(x)=x\sqrt{2x^2+11}\).',
 r'Find dy/dx of the following function in terms of parameter t: (a) x = 1 + t², y = t³ + 2t² + 1; (b) x = 3at² + 2, y = 6t⁴ + 9; (c) \(x=\frac{a(1-t^2)}{1+t^2},\quad y=\frac{2bt}{1+t^2}\); (d) \(x=\frac{3at}{1+t^3},\quad y=\frac{3at^2}{1+t^3}\).',
 r'At a certain factor, the total cost of manufacturing q units during the daily production run is C(q) = 0.2q² + q + 900 dollars. From experience, it has been determined that approximately q(t) = t² + 100t units are manufactured during the first t hours of a production run. Compute the rate at which the total manufacturing cost is changing with respect to time one hour after production begins.',
 r'Use implicit differentiation to perform dy/dx for the following functions: (a) x² + y² = 25; (b) xy(2x + 3y) = 2; (c) (x + y)³ + 3y = 3; (d) \(\frac1y+\frac1x=1\).',
 r'Arrange the following functions explicitly and implicitly to perform dy/dx: (a) x²y³ + y³ = 1; (b) xy + 2y = x²; (c) \(x+\frac1y=5\); (d) xy − x = y + 2.',
 r'Let \(\frac{u^2}{a^2}+\frac{v^2}{b^2}=1\), where a and b are nonzero constants. Find (a) du/dv; (b) dv/du.',
 r'Determine the slope of the tangent line to the curve 3x² − 7y² + 14y = 27 at the point P(−3, 0).',
 r'Suppose two motor boats leave from the same point at the same time. If one boat travels north at 15 miles per hour and the other boat travels east at 20 miles per hour. How fast will the distance between them be changing after 2 hours?'
]
STEPS=[
 [r'For a constant multiple of a power, differentiate the outer power, retain the inner expression, then multiply by its derivative.',r'(a) The inner derivative is 3x² − 4, so dw/dx = 4·5(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4) = 20(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4).',r'(b) dy/dx = (11/5)(4x − x³)¹⁰(4 − 3x²). Both (a) and (b) are defined and differentiable for all real x.',r'(c) Put r = ∛(1 − 3t²). For r ≠ 0, r³ = 1 − 3t² gives 3r²r′ = −6t, so \(du/dt=\frac{-2t}{[\text{∛}(1-3t^2)]^2}\).',r'The cube-root function is real for every t, including negative radicands. Its finite derivative excludes t = ±1/√3, where the radicand vanishes with a nonzero derivative.',r'(d) Write s = (3t + 1)⁻⁷. Then ds/dt = −7(3t + 1)⁻⁸·3 = −21/(3t + 1)⁸, with t ≠ −1/3.'],
 [r'(a) Use the product and chain rules: f′ = 6(2x − 5)²(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5)³.',r'Factor (2x − 5)². The remaining factor is 6(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5) = 40x − 67; thus f′ = (2x − 5)²(40x − 67).',r'(b) Quotient rule: \(f′=\frac{2(x+2)(x-1)-(x+2)^2}{(x-1)^2}=\frac{(x+2)(x-4)}{(x-1)^2}=\frac{x^2-2x-8}{(x-1)^2}\), x ≠ 1.',r'(c) For u = (2x − 5)/(x − 4), u′ = [2(x − 4) − (2x − 5)]/(x − 4)² = −3/(x − 4)².',r'Apply the outer fourth power: \(f′=4u^3u′=\frac{-12(2x-5)^3}{(x-4)^5}\), x ≠ 4.',r'(d) Product rule: \(f′=\sqrt{2x^2+11}+x\frac{2x}{\sqrt{2x^2+11}}=\frac{4x^2+11}{\sqrt{2x^2+11}}\). The radicand is positive for all real x.'],
 [r'Compute x′(t) and y′(t), then use dy/dx = y′(t)/x′(t) where x′(t) ≠ 0.',r'(a) x′ = 2t and y′ = 3t² + 4t. For t ≠ 0, dy/dx = (3t² + 4t)/(2t) = 3t/2 + 2.',r'At t = 0 both parameter derivatives vanish. Cancellation is not valid there. The two branches y = 2x − 1 ± (x − 1)^(3/2), x ≥ 1, have the same limiting slope 2 at (1,1).',r'(b) x′ = 6at and y′ = 24t³. Therefore dy/dx = 4t²/a for a ≠ 0, t ≠ 0. The key’s 12t²/a conflicts with the printed y = 6t⁴ + 9.',r'For a ≠ 0, eliminate t in (b): y = 2(x − 2)²/(3a²) + 9. Its tangent is horizontal at t = 0, although the parameter ratio is 0/0 there. For a = 0 the x-coordinate is constant and this formula is unavailable.',r'(c) x′ = −4at/(1 + t²)² and y′ = 2b(1 − t²)/(1 + t²)². Divide to obtain dy/dx = b(t² − 1)/(2at), for a ≠ 0 and t ≠ 0.',r'(d) x′ = 3a(1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)² and y′ = 3a(2t − t⁴)/(1 + t³)². Thus dy/dx = (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³), requiring a ≠ 0, t ≠ −1 and 1 − 2t³ ≠ 0.'],
 [r'The cost depends on q, while production q depends on time. Use dC/dt = (dC/dq)(dq/dt).',r'dC/dq = 0.4q + 1 dollars per production unit. dq/dt = 2t + 100 units/hour.',r'At t = 1 hour, q = 1² + 100·1 = 101 and dq/dt = 102 units/hour.',r'Thus dC/dq = 0.4·101 + 1 = 41.4 dollars/unit.',r'Multiply: dC/dt = 41.4·102 = 4222.8 dollars/hour. The total manufacturing cost is increasing at that instantaneous rate.'],
 [r'(a) Differentiate x² + y² = 25: 2x + 2yy′ = 0, giving y′ = −x/y where y ≠ 0. At y = 0 the circle has vertical tangents.',r'(b) Expand 2x²y + 3xy² = 2. Differentiate: 4xy + 2x²y′ + 3y² + 6xyy′ = 0.',r'Therefore \(y′=-\frac{4xy+3y^2}{2x^2+6xy}\), where the denominator is nonzero. The negative sign follows from moving the non-derivative terms to the other side.',r'(c) Differentiate (x + y)³ + 3y = 3: 3(x + y)²(1 + y′) + 3y′ = 0.',r'Collect y′ to obtain \(y′=\frac{-(x+y)^2}{(x+y)^2+1}\). The denominator is strictly positive for real x and y.',r'(d) Differentiate 1/y + 1/x = 1: −y′/y² − 1/x² = 0, hence y′ = −y²/x², with x ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0.',r'Check (d) explicitly: y = x/(x − 1), x ≠ 0,1, and y′ = −1/(x − 1)². This agrees with the answer key’s −y²/x².'],
 [r'(a) Factor y³(x² + 1) = 1, so y = (x² + 1)^(−1/3). Explicitly, y′ = −2x/[3(x² + 1)^(4/3)], all real x.',r'Implicitly, 2xy³ + 3y²(x² + 1)y′ = 0 gives y′ = −2xy/[3(x² + 1)]. Substituting the positive y above agrees with the explicit derivative.',r'(b) y(x + 2) = x² gives y = x²/(x + 2), x ≠ −2. Quotient differentiation yields y′ = (x² + 4x)/(x + 2)².',r'Implicitly, y + (x + 2)y′ = 2x gives y′ = (2x − y)/(x + 2), the same result after substitution.',r'(c) 1/y = 5 − x gives y = 1/(5 − x), x ≠ 5. Explicitly y′ = 1/(5 − x)². Implicitly 1 − y′/y² = 0 gives y′ = y², which agrees.',r'(d) y(x − 1) = x + 2 gives y = (x + 2)/(x − 1), x ≠ 1, and y′ = −3/(x − 1)².',r'Implicitly, y + xy′ − 1 = y′ gives y′ = (1 − y)/(x − 1), which agrees after substitution.'],
 [r'a and b are fixed nonzero constants; the curve is an ellipse in coordinates u and v.',r'(a) Treat u as a function of v. Differentiate: (2u/a²)(du/dv) + 2v/b² = 0.',r'Solve: du/dv = −a²v/(b²u), wherever u ≠ 0.',r'(b) Treat v as a function of u. Differentiate: 2u/a² + (2v/b²)(dv/du) = 0.',r'Solve: dv/du = −b²u/(a²v), wherever v ≠ 0. The two derivatives are reciprocal where both are finite and nonzero. At zero denominators, use the appropriate tangent direction instead.'],
 [r'Check the point: 3(−3)² − 7·0² + 14·0 = 27, so P is on the curve.',r'Differentiate: 6x − 14yy′ + 14y′ = 0.',r'Collect: (14 − 14y)y′ = −6x, giving y′ = 3x/[7(y − 1)] where y ≠ 1.',r'At P(−3,0), y′ = −9/(−7) = 9/7.',r'The requested slope is 9/7. As a learning extension, the tangent equation is y = (9/7)(x + 3).'],
 [r'Let x be the eastward distance, y the northward distance and z the separation. After t hours, x = 20t and y = 15t, in miles.',r'The right triangle gives z² = x² + y². Differentiating for t > 0: 2z dz/dt = 2x dx/dt + 2y dy/dt.',r'At t = 2, x = 40 miles, y = 30 miles and z = √(40² + 30²) = 50 miles.',r'Use dx/dt = 20 and dy/dt = 15 miles/hour: dz/dt = (40·20 + 30·15)/50 = 1250/50 = 25 miles/hour.',r'The separation is increasing at 25 miles/hour. Independently, z(t) = √((20t)² + (15t)²) = 25t for t ≥ 0, confirming that rate for every t > 0.']
]
ANSWERS=[r'(a) 20(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4); (b) (11/5)(4x − x³)¹⁰(4 − 3x²); (c) −2t/[∛(1 − 3t²)]², t ≠ ±1/√3; (d) −21/(3t + 1)⁸, t ≠ −1/3.',r'(a) (2x − 5)²(40x − 67); (b) (x² − 2x − 8)/(x − 1)²; (c) −12(2x − 5)³/(x − 4)⁵; (d) (4x² + 11)/√(2x² + 11). Use the domain restrictions in the steps.',r'(a) 3t/2 + 2; (b) 4t²/a; (c) b(t² − 1)/(2at); (d) (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³). Validity and exceptional parameter values are explained in the steps.','4222.8 dollars/hour, increasing.',r'(a) −x/y; (b) −(4xy + 3y²)/(2x² + 6xy); (c) −(x + y)²/[(x + y)² + 1]; (d) −y²/x². Apply the original relation and nonzero-denominator conditions.',r'(a) −2x/[3(x² + 1)^(4/3)]; (b) (x² + 4x)/(x + 2)²; (c) 1/(5 − x)²; (d) −3/(x − 1)². Explicit and implicit forms agree.',r'(a) du/dv = −a²v/(b²u), u ≠ 0; (b) dv/du = −b²u/(a²v), v ≠ 0.','Slope 9/7.','25 miles/hour, increasing.']
CLARIFICATIONS={2:'The key prints 12t²/a for Question 3(b); the source y = 6t⁴ + 9 has derivative 24t³, so the correct ratio is 4t²/a.'}
